    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A surprise storm an hour after you finish sealing feels like a disaster, and sometimes it is. Often it is not. Water-based driveway sealers go through a short stage when rain can rinse them off, a longer stage when it can only mark the surface, and then a point where they shrug it off. Knowing which stage your coat was in when the first drops hit tells you whether to do nothing, touch up a few spots, or scrub and recoat. Do not try to judge any of it while the drive is still wet.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Dry Hours Fresh Sealer Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Labels for water-based asphalt emulsion sealers commonly list a rain-free period somewhere between four and eight hours in warm, dry weather, and many recommend a full 24 hours for best results. Some fast-dry polymer products advertise shorter rain-safe times, and a few premium products list longer ones. The number on your specific pail is the one to follow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those times assume ideal conditions: roughly 70°F or warmer, low humidity, and sun or a light breeze. Cooler air, high humidity or heavy shade can easily double them. A coat that would be safe in four hours on a dry July afternoon might still be vulnerable after ten on a cool, damp September day. When in doubt, plan for 24 dry hours, and treat anything shorter as a calculated risk.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Rain-Safe Times, Warm and Dry</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-8 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common label minimum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Safer target</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stretch in cool, humid weather</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before driving on it</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Foot traffic and car traffic follow later than rain resistance. A coat can be rain-safe while still too soft for tires, which can scuff or pick up a fresh film, especially when turning. Most labels suggest waiting about 24 to 48 hours before driving on the surface. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">driveway sealer dry times</a> lays out each stage in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature drives these numbers as much as humidity. If you sealed on a cool day, even a light shower in the evening can do more harm than you expect. Our page on <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a> explains how air, pavement and overnight temperatures combine to slow or speed the cure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Rain Does at Each Stage of Curing
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Within the first hour or so:</strong> the sealer is still a liquid suspension. Steady rain can dilute it and wash much of it off, leaving streaks of brown or gray runoff along the edges and onto sidewalks, garage floors and lawn. The surface underneath may be only partly coated. This is the worst case, and usually means a full recoat once the weather clears.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Roughly one to four hours in:</strong> the top of the film has started to skin over, but the layer underneath is still soft. Rain at this stage tends to leave spots, pitting or a dull, uneven finish rather than washing the coat away. Light drizzle may only mark the surface. Heavy rain can still pull material off in areas where water collects or flows.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Roughly four to eight hours in, in good weather:</strong> most of the water has gone and the particles have begun to fuse. Rain now usually causes cosmetic issues at most, such as faint water spots or a slightly lighter patch where water pooled. Many coats at this stage look fine once the surface dries again. <strong>After 24 hours:</strong> a properly applied coat in reasonable weather should be unaffected.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time before rain</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely result</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical fix</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 1 hour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coat partly washed away, runoff stains</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean runoff, full recoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1-4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pitting, spots, thin areas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot or full recoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4-8 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Faint spots or haze, often none</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wait; touch up if needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24+ hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No damage expected</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those windows are approximate and assume warm weather. On a cool or humid day, shift each stage later. The most useful thing you can do right after a storm is note how many hours the coat had before rain started, then hold off on any decisions until the surface has fully dried.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Inspect the Driveway Once It Dries
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet sealer and dry sealer look very different, and rain-soaked sealer can look gray, milky or patchy for hours even when the coat is intact. Wait until the drive has dried completely, ideally a full sunny day, before you judge it. Many owners who panic at a gray, streaky driveway the evening of a storm find that it darkens back to an even black by the next afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then walk the drive slowly in good light. Look for <strong>bare or thin areas</strong> where the original asphalt shows through, <strong>pitting or crater marks</strong> from heavy drops, <strong>streaks or runs</strong> where water flowed, and <strong>a whitish or gray film</strong> on the surface. Check low spots and the edges closest to the street, since those are where water pools and flows.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quick scratch test helps. Drag a fingernail or the corner of a plastic card across a suspect area. If the coat feels firm and only the very top dulls, it is likely sound. If it smears, flakes or powders, the film did not cure properly and that section needs to be cleaned and recoated. Pour a little water on a few spots as well; on sound sealer it should bead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Judge it dry, not wet.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rain-wet sealer often looks gray and blotchy even when it is perfectly sound. Wait a full dry day before deciding whether you need to recoat anything, and test suspect areas with a fingernail and a splash of water.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Make a quick sketch or take photos of problem areas. Damage after rain is often patchy rather than uniform, with the high, well-drained parts of the drive fine and the low corners and edges affected. Knowing exactly where the problems are makes it easier to decide between spot repairs and a full recoat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runoff" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cleaning Up Runoff on Walks, Garage Floors and Lawn
          </h2>

          <p>
            If rain hit within the first hour or two, you may have brown or black streaks on concrete walks, the garage floor or the curb. Speed matters. Wet sealer residue is far easier to remove than cured residue. As soon as it is safe, rinse the concrete with a hose and scrub with a stiff broom and a degreasing detergent, working the residue away from storm drains toward a lawn or gravel area where possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once sealer residue has cured on concrete, removal gets harder. Owners often have success with a concrete degreaser and repeated scrubbing, or with a pressure washer on a fan tip, used carefully to avoid etching the surface. Some sealer manufacturers sell or recommend specific cleaners; check the pail or the company website. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> covers degreasers that also help here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not let sealer runoff flow into storm drains if you can avoid it. Many municipalities restrict pavement sealer discharge, and coal tar products in particular are regulated in a number of areas because of PAH concerns. A few sandbags or a rolled towel at the bottom of a sloped drive can divert flow during a storm. Wear gloves when handling residue, especially coal tar sealer, which is a skin irritant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grass and plants splashed with sealer usually recover once the residue is rinsed off, though a strip right at the drive edge may brown temporarily. Rinse affected lawn with plenty of water. Remove any sealer that has pooled in garden beds or gravel with a shovel rather than spreading it around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Repair Options, From Doing Nothing to Recoating
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Do nothing</strong> when the coat had several hours of good drying before rain and looks even once dry. Faint water spots often fade within days as sun and traffic even out the surface. Give it a week before deciding, unless you see bare asphalt or a soft film.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Spot recoat</strong> when damage is limited to low areas, edges or a few patches. Wait until the drive is completely dry, sweep the area, and scrub off any loose, chalky or flaking material with a stiff broom. Apply a thin coat to the damaged area and feather the edges into the surrounding sealer. The patch may look slightly different at first, but it usually blends within a few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Full recoat</strong> when the coat largely washed off or the scratch test shows widespread soft or powdery film. Scrub the whole drive with a stiff broom and water, rinse, and let it dry thoroughly, usually at least a full day in good weather. Then apply a fresh coat following the label's coverage rate. Do not apply new sealer over residue that is still soft or flaking; the new layer will only be as strong as what is under it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you do, pick your next weather window carefully. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> covers prep and application, and if you are choosing a replacement product, our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a> note each pick's dry time and rain-safe window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dew, Sprinklers and Other Water You Forgot About
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rain is not the only threat. <strong>Lawn sprinklers</strong> on a timer are a common culprit, often running at dawn on the morning after a sealing job. Turn the system off for at least 24 hours, and check whether any heads spray onto the drive edges. A neighbor's sprinkler can do the same damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dew and fog</strong> can mark a fresh coat, especially when sealing in late afternoon in spring or fall. When the pavement cools below the dew point overnight, condensation forms on the surface. On a coat that has not yet set, it can leave a dull haze or blotches. Finishing by early afternoon gives the film time to firm up before evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gutters and downspouts</strong> that discharge onto the driveway can send a concentrated stream across fresh sealer during even a short shower. Redirect them temporarily with an extension or a bucket. <strong>Melting frost or snow</strong> is rarely an issue in normal sealing season, but in late fall it can be.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, watch for car air-conditioning drips and pets. A parked car on a nearby section dripping condensate, or a dog crossing the drive, can leave marks on a fresh coat. Block off the drive with tape or cones until it is ready for traffic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="forecast" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Planning Around Rain So It Does Not Happen Again
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the hourly forecast, not just the daily percentage. A 30 percent chance of rain spread across a whole day says little about your six-hour window. Look for a block of at least 24 dry hours beginning when you plan to start, and check the radar the morning of. If showers are tracking toward you, wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pop-up summer storms are hard to predict, especially in the Southeast and Midwest. In those regions, sealing in the morning gives the coat the most drying time before the typical afternoon storm window. Spring and fall often offer more stable stretches, though cooler temperatures slow drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tarps and plastic sheeting are not a good fix for a surprise shower. They stick to soft sealer, trap moisture and leave wrinkle prints that look worse than any raindrop. If rain is coming and you have not started, do not start. If you are midway through, finish the section you are on to a clean edge, then stop.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your drive keeps getting caught out by weather, consider shifting the job to a more stable season. Our guide to <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">the best time of year to seal</a> covers seasonal patterns, and our page on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> can confirm whether the job even needs doing this year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contractor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. If a Contractor Sealed It
          </h2>

          <p>
            If a sealcoating company did the work and rain hit within a few hours, call them once the drive has dried and send photos. Many reputable contractors have a policy for weather damage, which may include a free touch-up or recoat if rain arrived inside a stated window. Others will argue the forecast was clear when they started. Either way, ask early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before hiring, ask about the rain policy in writing. A good contractor will know exactly how many dry hours their product needs and what they will do if weather interferes. Vague answers are a warning sign. Also ask which product they use and whether it is coal tar or asphalt based, since coal tar is restricted in some areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional sealcoating often uses commercial-grade products with more solids and additives than the home-center pails, but they still need dry weather to cure. A crew that seals in the rain or right before a storm is cutting corners. Our page on <a href="/asphalt-sealcoating/">asphalt sealcoating</a> explains the steps a proper crew follows, from crack repair to the final pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the receipt and note the date, product and weather. If problems show up weeks later, like peeling or powdering, that record helps you and the contractor figure out whether rain, temperature or application was the cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the damage while the drive is wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rain-soaked sealer often looks gray, milky or patchy even when the coat is fine. Making decisions in that state leads to unnecessary recoating. Wait at least a full dry day, then inspect in good light and use a fingernail scratch test and a water bead test on suspect areas.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating over soft or flaking residue</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new layer bonds only as well as what it sits on. Spreading fresh sealer over powdery or partly washed-off material traps a weak layer underneath that peels later. Scrub damaged areas with a stiff broom, rinse, and let the drive dry thoroughly before applying a new coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the sprinkler timer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An automatic sprinkler running at dawn the morning after sealing can mark or thin a fresh coat as effectively as a storm. Turn off irrigation for at least 24 hours, and check for heads that spray onto the edges of the drive. Ask neighbors about theirs if they overlap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Covering wet sealer with a tarp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plastic sheeting sticks to soft sealer, traps moisture and leaves wrinkles and marks that are worse than rain spots. If rain threatens before you start, wait. If you are midway, finish the current section to a clean edge and stop. Do not cover fresh work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting runoff cure on concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer streaks on walks and garage floors are easy to rinse while wet and hard to remove once cured. Act fast with a hose, stiff broom and degreasing detergent, and steer runoff away from storm drains toward lawn or gravel where possible.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many dry hours does sealer need before a shower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based asphalt sealers list a rain-free period of roughly four to eight hours in warm, dry weather, and many recommend 24 hours for best results. Cooler temperatures and high humidity can double that time. Check your specific product's label, and plan for at least 24 dry hours whenever you can.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the coat ruined if a storm hits an hour later?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Within the first hour or so, the sealer is still liquid, so steady rain can thin it and wash some of it off, leaving runoff stains and thin spots. Wait until the drive dries completely, then inspect. If the coat is patchy or bare in areas, clean off any loose residue and recoat once the weather clears.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I reseal right after rain damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not immediately. Let the driveway dry completely, usually at least a full sunny day. Then scrub off any loose or flaking material, rinse, and let it dry again. Applying new sealer to damp pavement or soft residue leads to poor adhesion. Choose a window with at least 24 dry hours forecast for the recoat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will dew ruin new driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Heavy dew can leave a dull haze or blotchy marks on a coat that has not set yet, especially when sealing late in the day in spring or fall. It rarely washes sealer away. Finishing by early afternoon gives the film time to firm up before evening condensation forms on the cooling pavement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can I tell if rain ruined the sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Once dry, look for bare or thin spots, pitting, streaks and whitish film. Scratch suspect areas with a fingernail. Firm sealer that only dulls slightly is sound; material that smears, flakes or powders did not cure properly. A splash of water should bead on good sealer and soak into failed areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I drive on the driveway after it rains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain does not shorten the wait for traffic. Most labels recommend 24 to 48 hours before driving, and rain can extend that by slowing the cure. Press a fingernail into the surface in a tire path; if it leaves a mark or feels tacky, keep vehicles off a while longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth sealing with showers due the next day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you have at least 24 dry hours between when you finish and when rain is expected, and the weather is warm and dry in the meantime. If the window is shorter or the forecast is uncertain, wait. A rescheduled weekend costs nothing, while a washed-out coat costs a full recoat.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

